            <ArticleTitle>Transient monoclonal gammopathy induced by Candida fungemia.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0046-8177(17)30431-8</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.humpath.2017.11.012</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 41-year-old woman was admitted for Candida fungemia. On hospital day 4, a routine complete blood count and peripheral smear showed circulating plasma cells. Initial work up showed an M-component on serum protein electrophoresis with 6% lambda-predominate plasma cells by flow cytometry. The patient was treated with intravenous antifungal therapy. Her six month follow up laboratory evaluation revealed resolution of the M-component and only rare polyclonal plasma cells in peripheral blood by flow cytometry. This case illustrates that transient monoclongal gammopathy can be induced by fungal infection. It is important to exclude a plasma cell neoplasm or a B-cell lymphoma and to follow the patient until resolution of abnormal findings.</AbstractText>
